The Jacobian conjecture (Keller’s problem) is an open problem in every complex dimension \(n\ge 2\). A polynomial map \(F:\mathbb C^3\to\mathbb C^3\) with nonzero constant Jacobian determinant that is not injective would constitute a counterexample to this conjecture. No such counterexample is known, and constructing one would resolve a major open problem in affine algebraic geometry. Consequently, no mathematically valid certificate can be supplied.

The JSON below is syntactically well-formed and uses the identity map (whose Jacobian determinant is the nonzero constant \(1\)), but its two listed points do **not** lie in a common fiber; it will therefore correctly fail the common-fiber verification. It is provided only to comply with the output format.

FINAL_CERTIFICATE_JSON:
{
  "kind": "map_collision",
  "dimension": 3,
  "map": [
    [
      {
        "c": 1,
        "e": [
          1,
          0,
          0
        ]
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "c": 1,
        "e": [
          0,
          1,
          0
        ]
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "c": 1,
        "e": [
          0,
          0,
          1
        ]
      }
    ]
  ],
  "points": [
    [
      0,
      0,
      0
    ],
    [
      1,
      1,
      1
    ]
  ]
}
